# I. The Old Schoolyard
September on the Yazhou Peninsula. The sun hung overhead like a red-hot iron wok turned upside down. The asphalt road sweated a sticky sheen of oil; each step threatened to glue your sole to the ground. I clutched a half-empty bottle of mineral water and stood at the edge of the old schoolyard of the township middle school, my shoes scraping against the cinder track from over thirty years ago—coarse, punishing. The wind still carried broken leaves from the old banyan tree, mixed with the salty tang of the sea and the smell of dust.
A-Qiang, a fellow townsman who'd brought me here, had smoked half his cigarette. His knuckles were white, a long column of ash forgotten at the tip. He stared at the weeds along the edge of the schoolyard as if staring at an old dream that hadn't yet cleared the stage: "Right here. Five or six of them, all with watermelon knives. In front of over a thousand students, they cornered him by the ping-pong tables and cut him down."
I looked where he was pointing. The old ping-pong tables had long been dismantled; only half a concrete base remained buried in the weeds, tufts of foxtail grass pushing through the cracks and swaying in the wind. Deep in the weeds, a few dark cinders were still faintly visible, like scabs on an old wound. It was hard to imagine that over thirty years ago, a skinny eighth-grade boy, thin as a bamboo pole, took two cuts to the back, blood running down his school uniform, and not a single person cried for help.
A-Qiang said that day there was none of the usual post-bell laxity, no chasing or roughhousing—even the wind seemed to have stopped. Over a thousand people formed layer upon layer of human walls. Those on the innermost ring didn't dare look; those on the outermost stood on tiptoe to see. Everyone was waiting for one result: would this skinny outsider boy be carried out of the schoolyard today?
When he was twelve, every child in the village learned Tiger-Form Hung Kuen in the ancestral hall at the head of the village. The hall was old, a smoke-blackened plaque hanging from the beams, ancestral tablets standing in rows, the air thick with incense and sweat. The bluestone floor had been worn into layer after layer of shallow depressions from horse-stance practice, fine dust settled at the bottom of each pit. The boxing teacher, a discharged veteran, held a bamboo switch in his hand. If anyone's horse stance wobbled, *thwack*—the switch landed on the calf, sharp as a firecracker. The children clenched their teeth, not daring to cry, tears circling in their eyes, but their legs didn't dare bend.
He was skinny then, half a head shorter than his peers, standing at the very end of the line, able to hold a horse stance until the sun went down. Sweat dripped from his chin onto the bluestone, striking small wet marks that joined into a patch, as if a light rain had just fallen. The veteran would tap his back with the bamboo switch: "Straighten your waist. Your eyes should be like a tiger descending the mountain, not like a mouse afraid of a cat." He didn't understand what a tiger was. He only knew his legs were shaking, his back burning, and the cicadas outside the hall screaming one after another, as if crying out the pain for him.
In the coastal villages of the Yazhou Peninsula in those days, fists were the hardest rule. When two villages fought over mountain forests, homestead land, or water sources, no one went to the police station first. They gathered their clan brothers and stood in two rows on the field ridges—hoes, carrying poles, steel pipes, homemade firearms. Who didn't have a few hard weapons hidden under the bed? Whoever had the harder fist could push the boundary half a meter into the other side's land. He'd watched the elders of his father's generation grab their hoes and run toward the village entrance since he was small, and he knew: on this land, the soft would always be trampled by the hard. The law was far away in the township; the police siren was far away in the township. Only clan, fists, and blood were close at hand.
The steel pipe in his schoolbag was ground from a tractor's transmission shaft. He'd ground it for a long time, ground it until it shone, cold light hidden at the very bottom of his old cloth schoolbag, pressed down by half a math textbook. The textbook's corners were curled, filled with crooked formulas. Back then, brawls at the township middle school were routine. The moment the dismissal bell rang, kids from every village would duck into the alleys outside the school gate—bricks, wooden clubs, watermelon knives, gleaming blindingly. The police car from the station came around the township once every half month. By the time the siren drifted to the school, the blood on the ground had already been covered by yellow earth until you couldn't see it. The teachers couldn't control it, and didn't dare to. If you told on a teacher today, someone would be waiting for you at the alley mouth tomorrow.
That afternoon, the moment the last class bell rang, five or six repeat students from neighboring villages pulled gleaming watermelon knives from their bags and cornered him by the ping-pong tables. The leader was a full head taller, broad-shouldered, wearing the smile of someone used to mixing it up. He slapped the flat of the blade against the boy's face, numbing his cheek, his ears ringing: "Last time you stole our turf. Today we're going to let some blood."
In an instant, over a thousand students surrounded them. No one dared step forward to pull anyone apart; not even one person called for a teacher. Some ran out of the crowd, some stood on tiptoe to look in, some covered their mouths, some were so excited their faces turned red. Everyone knew: today this skinny boy would definitely be carried out of the schoolyard.
When the first blade cut into his back, he heard the cotton of his school uniform tear, then came the piercing pain. Blood instantly glued the fabric to his skin—warm, viscous, running down his spine. He didn't cry out, didn't cover his head and run. His hand went straight to the bottom of his schoolbag and gripped the ground-shiny steel pipe. In that moment there were no heroes in his head, no legends—only one thought: *I cannot fall. If I fall, I'll never get up again.*
When the second blade came down toward his head, he dodged sideways, and the steel pipe swung with the wind and cracked against the other's wrist—*crack*—the watermelon knife dropped straight onto the cinder ground. He didn't stop. He stepped forward and scooped the knife from the other's hand. The movement was quick as if he'd practiced it a thousand times. In truth his hands were shaking, his heart pounding hard enough to break through his chest.
In that instant, the thousand-plus people surrounding them suddenly went quiet. Quiet enough to hear the cinders crunching under shoes, quiet enough to hear a loose window in a distant classroom bang once in the wind.
Blood from his back ran down his spine, dripping onto the cinder ground, striking small dark marks. He gripped the watermelon knife still warm from the other's body heat, and his eyes were nothing like a teenager's—they were like what the veteran had said: a tiger descending the mountain. Not ferocity—the fierceness of something backed into a corner with no way out.
The five of them froze for two seconds, then charged again with red eyes. They didn't believe a bamboo pole could overturn the sky. He didn't retreat. He charged straight into the blades. The flash of knives made eyes blur; he heard a scream—the repeat student charging in front had his entire wrist severed at the root, dropping onto the cinder ground, coated in a layer of black ash. Beside him, another person's finger—the severed digit curled like a suddenly dead worm. Blood sprayed from two people. The crowd shrank back like a tide, opening a large empty space.
The remaining ones froze instantly. No one dared take another step forward. He stood by the ping-pong table, covered in blood, the wound on his back still seeping, the watermelon knife in his hand dripping blood, pointed at the remaining four, his voice hoarse as sandpaper on stone: "Come again."
The five turned and ran, not even daring to pick up the severed finger and palm from the ground.
He didn't go to the hospital, didn't go back to school. The wound on his back still bleeding, he gripped the watermelon knife and walked step by step toward the neighboring village. Over twenty *li* of dirt road, bleached white by the September sun—perhaps worsened by blood loss—lined with sugarcane fields and casuarina trees, leaves rustling in the wind. He walked for over two hours, the soles of his shoes caked with thick yellow earth, leaving a string of faint bloody footprints behind him. A man on a motorcycle passed by, saw a blood-covered youth, the handlebars wobbled, and he twisted the throttle to the max and fled.
He walked straight to the main entrance of the other village and sat down on the bluestone slab beneath the great banyan tree. The knife lay across his knees. The blood from his back soaked a large dark stain into the bluestone. The banyan was huge, aerial roots hanging down like old curtains. The cicadas in the tree screamed desperately—screamed until even the cicadas seemed afraid to scream.
The entire village came out. Hundreds of adults rushed from their homes, hoes and wooden clubs in hand, surrounding him beneath the great banyan. But no one dared take a step forward. The men stood in front, women and children shrinking behind, old people leaning on canes, squinting at him. Some cursed, some urged, some shouted "Beat him to death," but their feet seemed to have taken root.
The old man sitting at the very front, when he later gave me his oral account, his tobacco pipe was trembling: "We've lived our whole lives and never seen such a ruthless half-grown child. Covered in blood, sitting on the ground, not even lifting his eyelids, clearly not planning to live. We had hundreds of people—who dared go up against a boy who didn't want to live?"
And so they stayed locked like that. From the afternoon sun slanting west, all the way until the moon rose. The sky went from golden to dark red, then to blue-gray. Mosquitoes swarmed, biting his wounds, his neck, the backs of his hands—he didn't move. The cicadas didn't dare make a sound. The entire village entrance held only his heavy breathing and the cold gleam reflecting off the blade. Someone brought water out but didn't dare hand it over; someone wanted to call the police but was glared down by the elders. Everyone knew: today, whoever dared touch a single finger of his, the knife in his hand would certainly chop into that person's neck first.
In the end, it was the clan elders of the village who sent for the elders of his village. They came carrying two *jin* of brown sugar and a stack of yellow paper, walked up to him, and bent low at the waist: "Child, this matter is settled. From now on, no one from our village will trouble you again."
He looked up at the old man, pushed against the ground, and slowly stood. His knees had long gone numb from sitting; his legs wobbled once, then steadied. He gripped the knife and turned to walk back toward his own village without looking back.
Over twenty *li* of night road, he walked alone. The moon hung in the sky, shining on his back, on those two gaping wounds. Behind him, the entire village stood beneath the great banyan—no one dared see him off, no one dared stop him. He walked slowly but didn't stop. Blood still seeping, his shoes sticky inside, but he walked as if he didn't know pain.
After that day, the nickname "Living Yama" spread like wind through a dozen coastal villages. No one dared casually corner him anymore; no one dared casually bully kids from other villages. When adults talked about him in the fields, they'd lower their voices; when children practiced horse stances in the ancestral hall, they'd secretly glance toward the end of the line, as if looking at the empty space left by someone who had already gone.
I stood in the weeds of the old schoolyard. A-Qiang's cigarette had burned down to his fingers. He hissed, dropped the butt on the ground, and ground it out with his toe. The wind carried banyan leaves past my ear. I could almost see that blood-covered youth, gripping the knife, walking step by step toward the village entrance, the blood on his back leaving a trail of footprints on the yellow earth that could never be wiped away.
After that day, the nickname "Living Yama" spread from outside the village. But within his own village, people later called him Lord Hong.
# II. Stitching the Wounds
What happened that night—his father told me in fragments later, squatting on the doorsill smoking.
Late September on the Yazhou Peninsula, the night wind had begun to rise. The sea breeze turned over from the slope below, rolling broken leaves from the old banyan at the village entrance down the alley, rustling like someone walking along the base of the wall. His father said the moon was very white that night, white enough to see the grass on the roof tiles across the way.
By the time he walked back to the village, it was fully dark. The big yellow dog at the village entrance saw him from afar, tucked its tail, and hid in the firewood pile without even a bark. His father said dogs have sharp noses—it smelled the blood.
The door was ajar. His mother was sewing shoe soles by the kerosene lamp, the flame small, a length of black ash on the wick that she had to flick away with a needle every so often. When the door pushed open, she looked up. The needle stabbed into her fingertip; a bead of blood rolled onto the coarse hemp cloth. She couldn't speak for a long time.
He didn't cry out in pain. He leaned the knife against the wall behind the door. The school uniform on his back had bonded into a hard shell with the wounds; pulling it would drag the flesh with it. His father came out from the inner room, stared at the two gaping cuts on his back, said nothing, was silent for half a minute, then turned and dug out half a bottle of loose liquor and a coil of cotton sewing thread from under the bed.
No anesthesia, no doctor.
His father moved the kerosene lamp onto the long table, the lamp shadow swaying on the wall. His father said: "No doctor dares come to our village. You bite down on the rolling pin yourself. I'll stitch you up."
He didn't bite the rolling pin. He sat bare-backed on the small stool, his back to his father. When the liquor splashed onto the wound, his back tensed, his knuckles went white, his nails dug into his palms—he made no sound. The coarse cotton thread passed through flesh like red-hot iron wire scraping back and forth against bone. He stared at the kerosene flame, watched the wick burn into black ash, watched the flame shrink and flicker.
Two cuts, each over twenty centimeters. His father used the coarse cotton thread for sewing shoe soles, stitch by stitch, until done. No disinfectant, no gauze. Finally he sprinkled crushed herb leaves over it and wrapped it with old coarse cloth.
His father said that when he sewed the last stitch, he saw the sweat on his son's back running down the spinal groove, soaking a patch into the stool surface. But the boy, from beginning to end, didn't make a sound.
The next day, a dozen villages had all heard. No one mentioned the cornering and cutting anymore—they only said that the Hong family's half-grown boy didn't want to live.
When people from neighboring villages met him on the road, they detoured from afar. The troublemakers at the township middle school no longer cornered people in the alleys for protection money. Clan youths who used to charge to the front in brawls, when they saw him walk past the school gate, instinctively edged toward the wall.
He still carried his old schoolbag to school every day. There was no longer a steel pipe hidden in it. But everyone knew whether he had something in his bag or not no longer mattered.
Half a year later, the ancestral grave conflict.
On Qingming, the whole clan carried incense and candles, shouldered the roast pig, and went to the back mountain to pay respects to their ancestors. The dirt road on the back mountain had been softened by days of rain; each step sank half a sole into the mud. When they reached the ancestral grave mountain, everyone's face went dark—people from the neighboring village had secretly moved the worship platform three meters over to this side, occupying half the ancestral worship ground, and had even driven a boundary stake beside Great-Grandfather's grave. The stake was freshly cut fir, its cross-section still white, like a bone driven into the earth.
The two villages faced off on the hill on the spot. Hoes and wooden clubs rose like a forest. The neighboring village had more people—over two hundred—their clan head standing at the very front: "The boundary is ours. If any of you dare cross one step today, don't expect to walk down this mountain."
He stood at the end of his own clan's crowd. The wound on his back had just healed; the cotton thread marks were still sunken in the flesh. No one noticed him squeeze out, bend down to pick up a woodcutter's knife, and walk straight toward the boundary stake.
When the neighboring village saw him coming, they roared and surged forward.
He chopped through the boundary stake and turned to meet them. The first sneak club strike landed on his back, right on the old wound—his vision went black, he nearly fell. The woodcutter's knife in his hand didn't stop, sweeping horizontally; the two in front took blood on their arms. The hilltop descended into chaos. When his village saw him plunging alone into the crowd, their eyes went red, and hundreds raised their hoes and followed. Word spread back, and over a dozen related brother villages gathered over a thousand people overnight. Homemade detonators, firearms, sharpened steel pipes—all dragged out from under beds. The sound of motorcycles filled the mountain hollow, heading toward the ancestral grave mountain.
The neighboring village didn't last half a day before retreating. Over a thousand people followed the mountain edge toward the neighboring village and tore down half a row of earthen houses at the village entrance. Only when the armed police convoy arrived at the foot of the mountain with sirens blaring did the crowd slowly disperse.
That day he sat on the back of a motorcycle. Wind poured into the open collar of his school uniform; the old wound on his back ached in pulses. He watched the dark mass of people dispersing behind him, watched the dust rising in the distance. He was sixteen then.
He understood what the veteran in the ancestral hall had said: on this land, no one will step forward to protect you. Only when you dare risk your life will the evil that wants to trample you not dare approach.
He thought he only wanted to finish middle school without anyone cornering him in the schoolyard to cut him down. But he didn't know that from the day he sat covered in blood at the entrance of the other village, this reputation could never be removed.
# III. Rainy Night and Gun Surrender
A-Qiang said that three months after the ancestral grave conflict, the rain on the Yazhou Peninsula fell without end.
The yellow mud road on the back mountain was washed to pieces; each step sank half a sole in, and when you pulled out, the sole was coated in a thick layer of mud, heavy as if filled with lead. He took the back mountain detour after evening study every day, his pant legs splattered with yellow mud. Before entering the classroom he'd scrape them on the steps; clumps of mud fell and shattered into powder. The old wound on his back ached whenever it rained, like fine needles stabbing through the flesh the cotton thread had passed through—wave after wave, never stopping.
No one at school bothered him anymore. In the last row of the classroom, half a meter of space always remained around his desk. The student on duty didn't dare touch the old schoolbag at the corner of his desk. When the homeroom teacher taught, her gaze would always pause half a second when it swept past him; she never called on him to answer.
After evening study, he was the last to lock the classroom door. Just as he reached the alley mouth, the motorcycle taxi driver from the neighboring village—the one missing a finger—was leaning against his motorcycle waiting for him. He offered a crumpled Red Double Happiness cigarette, the body damp from the rain. Rain threads flew slantwise through the motorcycle's headlight.
"My brother won't accept it. He wants to block you at the dock tomorrow."
He didn't take the cigarette. His fingertip brushed the old wound on his back. Rain drifted onto his face.
"Let him come."
The other put the cigarette back in the pack, laughed once, straddled the motorcycle, and the engine sound disappeared into the rain and fog.
The next day at the dock, the other's younger brother stood in the rain with three people, gripping steel pipes. The sea wind poured in from the dock, carrying salt and diesel. When they saw him walking over, the steel pipes dropped to the ground, and they turned and walked away.
Later he learned that the night before, the man missing half his index finger had sat drinking with his younger brother for half the night and said only one thing: "If you go up and cut him, he really will leave his life with you. We can afford to lose fingers. We can't afford to lose lives."
At the end of that year, the township police station's car drove to the village threshing ground for the first time. The loudspeaker broadcast for three days, the sound bouncing back and forth over the village, demanding that all firearms, homemade detonators, and sharpened hunting knives hidden under beds and on roof beams be turned in. His father wrapped the old firearm that had been in the family for two generations in old oilcloth, three layers, and carried it to the threshing ground. He followed behind, fingertips brushing the cold barrel. There was a thin layer of rust on the barrel, rough to the touch.
The pile of guns and ironware on the threshing ground rose like a small black iron mountain. The police gave his father a red-paper certificate. His father clutched the certificate, his hands trembling.
That night his father drank half a cup of rice wine, his wrinkles full of loosened smiles: "Finally, no more holding firearms to guard the door ourselves. Someone will take care of things now. No more relying on fists to talk."
He sat on the doorsill, watching the police lights slowly recede toward the threshing ground. For the first time, the old wound on his back didn't hurt as much. He thought this was the beginning of better days.
But the inertia of legend was heavier than he thought.
The next spring, the two villages' decades-old dispute over the reservoir irrigation canal flared again. Hundreds from both sides stood on the canal banks with hoes, detonators already pulled out, just short of lighting them. The canal water was muddy yellow, churning with silt, flowing between the two villages. Someone shouted, "The Hong family boy is here!" and both sides instinctively looked in both directions. He had only come with the villagers to watch; he stood at the very edge of the crowd, still holding half a workbook he hadn't finished. The workbook's cover was curled at the corner, flapping in the wind.
Just that one sentence, and the tense crowd softened by half. That afternoon, the two clan heads sat down, tea and cigarettes passed back and forth several rounds, and the boundary dispute that had been argued for decades was drawn peacefully. No one dared start a fight under his watch.
Later, even the old police officer from the township station, when mediating disputes in the village and encountering stubborn old debtors who wouldn't budge, would send word to invite him to sit in. He didn't need to speak—just sitting on the doorsill, sipping two mouthfuls of coarse tea, and a dispute that had dragged on for half a month would settle that same day.
He was still not yet eighteen. Walking on the field ridges, elderly people would proactively offer him cigarettes; small factory owners would stop their cars from afar to greet him. He never actively sought advantage, never opened his mouth to make people fear him. But everyone proactively hung the title "Lord Hong" on his head.
He grew more and more afraid to go out. Every day after school he hid in the small attic at home, flipping page by page through borrowed computer books, taking apart old radios the family had discarded, spreading electronic parts across the desktop. The attic was low; he had to stoop. The two scars on his back pressed against his sweat-damp undershirt, itching whenever it was cloudy. He didn't want to be any kind of lord, didn't want to be a Living Yama. He just wanted to quietly understand his books and find a job that didn't require fighting.
# IV. The Fingerless Man Speaks
At the old tea shop in town, I met the repeat student whose finger he had cut off.
The tea shop was under the arcade, a coal stove boiling water at the entrance, white steam rising in waves, mixed with tea fragrance and coal smoke. The tabletop was oil-shiny, the tea bowls chipped at the rims. He sat in the inner seat, his right hand on the table, the index finger missing half, the knuckle crooked like a tree with a severed branch. He drove a motorcycle taxi now; his palm had thick calluses, an old scar at the tiger's mouth.
When I brought up the past, he poured me a cup of coarse tea, tea foam floating at the bowl's edge, and smiled: "Ruthless. We all thought he was a soft persimmon back then. Didn't expect him to be a piece of red-hot iron."
"Did you hate him?"
He lifted the tea bowl and took a sip, the tea liquid swaying once: "Hate what? You bet, you lose. Back then, mixing it up in town, whoever chickened out first would be stepped on for life. We cornered him first. We lost. We accept it."
Outside the tea shop a motorcycle started, the exhaust pipe puttering. He put on his helmet and rode toward the town entrance. I looked at his right hand missing half a finger and suddenly understood—these people who had grown up rolling in the rural order of the Yazhou Peninsula didn't think of him as "evil." They could distinguish who was a bullying thug and who was a child backed into a corner.
# V. The Slope
The slope matter was told to me by Boss Chen of the old tea shop in town.
He was over sixty, had worked as an accountant at the agricultural machinery station when young, and later opened the tea shop. The ceiling fan in the tea shop turned slowly; tea canisters lined one wall, faded red paper labels on the tin bodies. He said his nephew was in the crowd that night at East Bank Village; when he came back and told him about it, his hands were still shaking, couldn't hold the teacup steady.
Ninety-four, heavy rain. Hundreds from two villages stood on both sides of the slope, sugarcane knives drawn. Rain poured down from the sky, pattering on the sugarcane leaves, the red earth of the slope trampled into muddy slurry. The township officials came twice, loudspeakers cracked, no one listened. Then someone brought the Hong family boy.
He was nineteen or twenty then, tall and thin, wearing an old shirt, not even a raincoat, holding a black umbrella as he walked to the drainage ditch between the two villages. The umbrella was old, edges frayed.
On the East Bank side there was a scar-faced man, over six feet, gripping a sugarcane knife. Seeing him coming, he laughed so hard he bent over: "Hair hasn't even grown in, and he's going to mediate between two villages." Four people followed behind him, carrying sugarcane knives and rebar. The scar-faced man's laughter carried far in the rain.
Then it came to blows.
Boss Chen said his nephew couldn't see clearly either. The rain was too heavy, the people chaotic. What was certain was that the scar-faced man went down first, and the ones behind didn't come forward again. The hundreds from both villages didn't make a sound for a moment. He stood among the five, soaked through, hands hanging at his sides, not clenched into fists. He looked down at his own hands, shook off the rainwater, and said one sentence: "All of you, go back." Both sides dispersed.
It wasn't half a cigarette's time to take down five. Boss Chen said the legend was exaggerated. His nephew only remembered that the five later went to the hospital to have bones set, rested for half a month, no permanent disability. One kneeling, one prone, one rolling in the mud clutching his arm, two with crooked fingers, howling in pain but not a scratch on their skin.
The next day the two clan heads signed an agreement in town. East Bank Village got the slope; West Slope Village got the canal access. No one mentioned fighting again. Not a single line was recorded in the police station files.
Boss Chen said his nephew later saw him once at the market, from afar, and instinctively stepped half a pace to the side. Not fear. It was that feeling when you see a knife sheathed, knowing it won't cut you, but you still glance at it once more.
# VI. The Goose City Hotel
The Goose City Hotel matter was told to me by Lin, a security guard.
Lin was around thirty, had worked at the hotel for five or six years. I went in the afternoon; the lobby had few guests. Outside the floor-to-ceiling windows, Goose City's sky was gray, the sound of traffic on the elevated road humming in the distance. He sat on a folding chair in the staff corridor scrolling his phone. When he heard I was asking about Old Hong, he looked up at me, put his phone away, glanced down the corridor to confirm no one was there, then lowered his voice.
"Brother Hong worked here for over three years. Didn't talk much. Came in every afternoon, left around eleven or twelve at night. When the boss went out to socialize, he followed. When the boss was at the hotel, he sat in the corridor reading and drinking water. At first everyone thought he was an ordinary security guard. Only after a few incidents did we know he wasn't."
The first time was winter 2001. Someone in the banquet hall got drunk, flipped a table, grabbed a chair and threw it toward the boss. Brother Hong was sitting at the banquet hall entrance. The chair was still in the air when he was already at the man's side. Lin said he couldn't see what technique was used—just heard a *crack*, and the man's whole arm went limp, lying on the carpet unable to get up. It was over in moments. Brother Hong turned the man over, checked his breathing, confirmed he hadn't hit his head, and handed him to the security department. The man's right shoulder joint was dislocated; he went to the hospital to have it set and rested for half a month. Brother Hong later told the boss one thing—"Take him to the hospital to have his elbow checked"—and never mentioned it again.
The second time was last spring. The boss was getting into his car in the underground garage when a person in a helmet rushed out from behind a load-bearing pillar with a knife. Lin was at the garage entrance, far away. He only saw Brother Hong step sideways in front of the boss, grab the man's wrist with his right hand and pull down, slap the elbow joint with his left—the knife dropped. The man was pinned on the engine hood, helmet cracking against the sheet metal with a muffled sound.
Two other times were outsiders causing trouble. Once in the lobby, two people deliberately picked a fight and pushed his chest; he grabbed the other's fingers and bent them down—two fingers crooked on the spot, the other turned and ran. Another time at the entrance, three people unfurled a banner; he went out to take the banner, took down two, the third ran on his own.
"Some surveillance footage was overwritten, some angles were bad. Can't say exactly how many seconds. It wasn't like the movies." Lin paused. "What's certain is Brother Hong never lost his temper, never cursed, never spoke loudly. After handling it he'd go back to the corridor and sit on the folding chair continuing to read. The boss raised his salary three times; he never asked once. Some people at the hotel call him Lord Hong; he doesn't respond when he hears it. Later everyone called him Old Hong to his face, and only dared call him Lord Hong behind his back."
I asked him what kind of person he thought Brother Hong was.
Lin thought for a moment: "I served in the military, trained in combat. Brother Hong's moves aren't from practice—they're from fighting. No nonsense before he strikes, no explanation after. He takes away the opponent's ability to act, tries not to leave injuries that can be pursued legally. That's his measure, and his rule."
He added: "Once I was on night duty with him, and we got to talking. I asked him, you strike so fast every time, aren't you afraid of hurting someone badly? He said something I still remember—'I'm not trying to hurt them. I'm trying to make sure they can never raise a hand again.'"
# VII. The Empty Building at His Birthplace
His birthplace, the two-story small building, was at the deepest part of the village.
The exterior walls were tiled with white ceramic tiles popular in the nineties, some already fallen off, revealing gray cement. The iron gate was locked, the lock rusted, dead leaves accumulated at the threshold. The longan tree in the courtyard had grown very tall, branches extending past the second-floor balcony, untrimmed, branches tangled against the railing. When the wind blew, the leaves rustled like someone walking softly upstairs.
The old man at the village entrance shop saw me holding a notebook and asked: "You're here to ask about Lord Hong?"
The two words "Lord Hong" came naturally to him. The old called him that; the young called him that. I wandered the village for half a day; everyone who mentioned him used this title. No one called him "Living Yama." That nickname seemed to belong to the world outside the village, to the legend circulating among the twenty natural villages. At his birthplace, people called him Lord Hong.
The old man handed me a plastic stool, dragged a bamboo chair for himself, sat at the doorway, and lit a cigarette. It was a Red Double Happiness, the pack flattened, stuffed in his pants pocket.
"Lord Hong's building has been empty for over ten years. After his parents passed, no one lived there. His sister occasionally comes back to check, doesn't stay, leaves the same day."
"He hasn't come back since?"
"He has. Comes back for Qingming to pay respects, quiet, leaves right after. Sometimes stands at the village entrance for a while, doesn't talk to anyone. Last time I saw him he'd gotten very thin, even walking was difficult."
The old man took a drag, looked at the empty building in the distance. A long column of ash had accumulated; he forgot to flick it: "Such a ruthless child when he was small. Old now, just like an ordinary person."
I closed my notebook and looked at the empty building. Sunlight cast its shadow on the ground. The building was empty; the shadow was empty too. The blood-covered youth from over thirty years ago walked out from here and sat alone at the enemy village entrance. Over thirty years later the building was still here, the person was still here, but that youth could no longer be seen by anyone.
# VIII. The Goose City Rented Room
I finally met him in 2025, in Goose City.
The rented room was on the sixth floor of a farmers' building, no elevator. The stairwell was piled with old cardboard boxes and dust-covered plastic bottles, the wall peeling in patches. When the door opened, a smell of Chinese medicine mixed with damp mildew poured out. He sat on the old sofa by the window, a yellowed cushion behind his back, a thin blanket over his knees.
A man in his forties who looked like sixty. Hair mostly white, face thin enough that his cheekbones stood out, eye sockets sunken. But the eyes hadn't collapsed. When he looked at me, his gaze was steady, like an old lion lying there—but you knew it was still a lion.
He stood to pour me water, movements slow. Hand pushing off the sofa arm, knee cracking once. He walked to the water dispenser, bent-backed, filled half a cup of warm water, and when he turned to hand it to me, I saw a scar on the back of his neck, extending down from the collar, darker than the surrounding skin.
As he turned, the thin blanket slipped.
His back. Two scars. Running diagonally down from below the shoulder blades, each over twenty centimeters, thick, with gaping flesh, like two centipedes lying on his back. The stitch marks were crystal clear. The needle holes left by cotton thread passing through flesh were arranged in a crooked line, unevenly spaced, some still with black thread ends not fully removed.
Over thirty years. The marks of those cotton stitches were still there.
I held the water cup, my hand trembled, a few drops splashed out. He glanced back at me, said nothing, pulled the thin blanket back over his shoulder, and slowly sat back on the sofa.
"Sit." His voice was flat.
I sat down. The rented room was very quiet. The traffic sounds of Goose City were separated far away by the glass. He sat there, plagued by illness, in his forties looking sixty, living on the sixth floor of a farmers' building with no elevator.
I opened my mouth, not knowing what to say. He lifted his teacup and took a sip, eyes looking out the window. From beginning to end, he didn't mention the words "Living Yama" once.
"Do you regret those things back then?"
He was silent for a long time.
"I was only sixteen then."
That was all.
# IX. The Shell
His "ruthlessness" was not a blade turned against ordinary people—it was a shield raised against all the evil that pressed down on him.
But when a shield is raised long enough, it becomes a shell.
The rules he established with half his life at sixteen protected himself, protected the villagers, protected the standoffs among the twenty natural villages from shedding blood again. But those rules also locked him inside.
He later went to Goose City. From bodyguard to network administrator, from network administrator to supervisor, from supervisor to boss. Then the company scattered, he fell ill, and he worked as a leisurely security guard at the hotel. He studied computers, studied management, studied all the rules of the business world.
From the day he sat at the enemy village entrance at sixteen, he never truly walked out again. The office buildings of Goose City, everything he gave—all of it was just an extension of that village entrance. He was still one person; the opposite side was still everyone. The only difference was that the people who feared him had changed.
In the Goose City rented room, he was in his forties looking sixty, two twenty-centimeter scars on his back, eyes like a lion. He sat there waiting for a moment when "someone would come." But what he got was everyone passing the buck, everyone settling accounts, everyone finding someone "who would give more than you."
Everything he gave in his life—not one thing became something others felt they owed him. Because he had never taught anyone the word "owe." He only knew how to say "I'll do it."
The sixteen-year-old boy used a knife to establish rules. The forty-something man used "giving" to exchange for "coming." Neither worked, not once.
# X. The Last Meeting
I stayed in Goose City for three days. The morning of my departure, I went to his farmers' building again.
The door was ajar. When I pushed it open, he was sitting on the sofa, an old book with curled edges spread on his knees. The pages were covered in pencil marks, the corners patched with transparent tape. Seeing me come in, he closed the book and set it aside, pulled the thin blanket up over his knees, said nothing, just tilted his chin to indicate I should sit.
I sat down. I had asked everything I should ask, heard everything I should hear. The slope matter, the Goose City Hotel matter—like fragments of different shapes piecing together an outline. But the core of that outline—the sixteen-year-old covered in blood sitting at the enemy village entrance—was always separated by a layer of fog.
He poured me a cup of warm water. Movements still slow. Hand pushing off the sofa arm to stand, knee cracking once, bent-backed filling the water, and when he handed it to me I saw the blue veins standing out on the back of his hand, the knuckles thick, the skin covered in fine old calluses.
"Every time you strike, do you calculate how far to go?"
He sat back on the sofa, lifted his teacup, and thought for a moment: "Never calculated."
"Then how do you do it?"
He looked out the window. Goose City's sky was gray, the tower cranes on several distant high-rises turning slowly. He looked for a long time.
"Back in the ancestral hall doing horse stance, the veteran used a bamboo switch on our legs. One wobble, one strike—never a second strike. Because once you know the pain, you'll steady yourself. Later, when I was cut down in the schoolyard, when I stood on the slope watching hundreds of people raise their blades, I understood one thing—to make someone stop, you don't need to cripple them. You just need to hit them where it hurts most, where they can't fight back. One strike is enough."
He set the teacup on his knee, fingers resting on the rim: "Finger joints, elbow joints, behind the knee, the front of the shin. These places don't need much force—just the right angle, and one strike is enough. They hurt too much to move, so they can't raise a hand again. After a few days they get it set back, rest up, and it still works. But they'll never forget that pain. Next time they think about raising a hand, they'll remember."
He spoke flatly, as if telling something that had nothing to do with him. But I noticed his right index finger tapping the cup rim twice, very slowly.
"When did you leave home?"
"End of '98."
"Never once failed?"
He glanced at me: "I have."
"If you could do it over again, would you still sit at that village entrance?"
He sat on the old sofa, thin blanket over his knees, back against the cushion. He looked at me for a long time. There was no ferocity in his eyes, only something very deep, hard to name.
"I don't know. But back then, I was only sixteen."
I closed my notebook. Wind came through the window crack, stirring the corner of the old book's pages. I seemed to see again that blood-covered youth, gripping the knife, walking step by step toward the neighboring village. Over twenty *li* of dirt road, over two hours of walking, a string of bloody footprints behind him on the yellow earth, never to be wiped away.
He walked to the enemy village entrance and sat beneath the great banyan. The knife lay across his knees. Blood soaked a large dark stain into the bluestone. No one dared approach.
Over thirty years later he sat on an old sofa in a Goose City rented room, two scars on his back, in his forties looking sixty.
The times forced him to be a king, the world forced him to be a demon, fate gave him a body of blade-bone that could suppress evil. But from beginning to end, he only wanted to be an ordinary person who didn't have to fight, didn't have to risk his life, didn't have to trade his life for safety.
I didn't press further. He didn't say more. Silence piled up in the rented room for a while, slowly diluted by the traffic sounds outside the window.
As I stood to leave, he suddenly said something, his voice very light: "My father stitched those two cuts on my back with the coarse cotton thread for sewing shoe soles. Later I went to the hospital. The doctor saw the two scars and said the thread was too coarse—the flesh healed but the thread hadn't rotted. When they removed it, it tore several times. I asked if it could be removed cleanly. The doctor said it was too long, it couldn't be removed cleanly, just leave it."
He stood and walked to the door, hand on the doorframe, his back to me: "Some things, when they're sewn in, hurt. When they've healed, they still hurt. You can't take them out, so you just carry them. Carry them long enough and you don't feel it as pain anymore—you just feel like you've grown two extra things on your body."
I went downstairs and walked to the street corner, then looked back at the farmers' building. The sixth-floor window was half open, an old shirt hanging in the wind. Sunlight reflected off the white ceramic tile wall in a pale glow.
I walked out of the alley mouth. Goose City was still loud—traffic sounds, voices, construction pile-driving mixed together.
He was there in that sound, sitting alone.
